[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Advanced Concurrency and Asynchronous Programming in Java"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern high-throughput applications demand efficient execution models that go beyond traditional thread-per-request blocking paradigms. This tutorial explores CompletableFuture, Reactive Programming concepts, and Java's Project Loom Virtual Threads, demonstrating how they solve scalability challenges."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Asynchronous Composition with CompletableFuture"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"CompletableFuture represents a future result of an asynchronous computation, providing a massive API for non-blocking composition, combining, and exception handling compared to the legacy Future interface."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"CompletableFuture<String> fetchUser = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> {
    // Simulate remote database call
    return "User123";
});

CompletableFuture<String> processOrder = fetchUser.thenCompose(userId -> 
    CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> "Order for " + userId)
);

processOrder.thenAccept(System.out.println).join();"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Reactive Programming Core Concepts"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Reactive programming is an asynchronous programming paradigm concerned with data streams and the propagation of change. It relies on four primary pillars defined by the Reactive Manifesto:"},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Responsive: The system reacts in a timely manner if at all possible."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Resilient: The system stays responsive in the face of failure."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Elastic: The system stays responsive under varying workload."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Message Driven: Reactive systems rely on asynchronous message-passing to establish a boundary between components."},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"A crucial feature of reactive streams (such as Project Reactor or RxJava) is backpressure, which allows consumers to signal producers how many items they can handle, preventing memory overflow."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"3. High Concurrency with Virtual Threads (Project Loom)"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Introduced as a standard feature in Java 21, Virtual Threads are lightweight threads that drastically reduce the effort of writing, maintaining, and observing high-throughput concurrent applications. Unlike platform threads mapped to OS threads, millions of virtual threads can run concurrently on a small pool of OS threads."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"try (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
    IntStream.range(0, 10_000).forEach(i -> {
        executor.submit(() -> {
            Thread.sleep(Duration.ofSeconds(1));
            return i;
        });
    });
} // Executor waits for all virtual threads to complete"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary and Best Practices"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choose CompletableFuture for straightforward asynchronous task composition. Use Reactive Programming when dealing with infinite, event-driven streams requiring strict backpressure. Adopt Virtual Threads to simplify blocking I/O applications without rewriting code into complex callback chains or reactive pipelines."}]
